## Summary

Make the existing RIPR product path singular across the public help and first-hour documentation:

```text
setup / diagnose       -> ripr doctor
inspect one change     -> ripr check
guided repo adoption   -> ripr pilot
repair one named gap   -> ripr agent repair --phase before|after
compose PR evidence    -> ripr first-pr
adopt advisory CI      -> ripr init --ci github
advanced reference     -> ripr help --all
```

Closes #2930.

## What changed

- align `ripr --help` and `ripr help --all` around the same task hierarchy;
- make `ripr agent repair` the primary repair path while retaining lower-level agent commands for explicit control and debugging;
- make `ripr check` the ordinary first-value command in the README and Quickstart;
- retain `ripr pilot` as guided repository adoption and `ripr first-pr` as PR-evidence composition;
- add `docs/COMMAND_HIERARCHY.md` as the bounded human authority consumed later by #1613;
- add a focused drift test that pins the task roles and advanced-command retention.

## User impact

A first-time user no longer receives conflicting answers from top-level help, README, Quickstart, and agent help about whether the primary path is `pilot`, `first-pr`, lower-level packet commands, or the dedicated repair driver.

No command is renamed or removed. Existing advanced and compatibility surfaces remain available.

## Release disposition

This is the bounded front-door coherence candidate described by #2930. It changes help, documentation, and a source-text drift test only. It does not alter analyzer behavior, output schemas, support tiers, LSP protocol, gate authority, packaging, or the durable repair-attempt work in #2927.

Admission to the `0.11.0` selected claim set remains an explicit #2766 decision. This PR does not make itself release-required.

## Proof

Branch review confirms:

- the default help remains within the existing 40-line envelope;
- existing help routes (`doctor`, `check`, `first-pr`, `lsp`, `init`, `help --all`) remain present;
- exhaustive help now includes `ripr agent repair` explicitly;
- the new test pins the canonical task routes and retains all lower-level agent command names;
- the branch is based directly on current `main` and contains no analyzer or authority change.

Hosted exact-head checks are the execution authority for formatting, compilation, tests, documentation contracts, and repository policy.

## Non-goals

- no command rename or removal;
- no top-level `ripr repair` alias;
- no durable attempt identity or resume;
- no typed `help --json` or workflow catalog;
- no automatic edit, verification-command execution, mutation execution, release, tag, publication, signing, marketplace, or credential action.

## Relationships

- #1613 — typed command and workflow catalog after this prose-level alignment;
- #2927 — durable repair transaction after `0.11.0`;
- #2928 — VS Code shell over that shared transaction;
- #2766 / #2893 — candidate-relative release disposition.

## Summary

Begin #2927 by giving every successful `ripr agent repair --phase before` invocation one durable operational identity and an attempt-specific evidence directory.

This is the first post-`0.11.0` implementation slice. It deliberately stops at the `awaiting_edit` boundary; finishing through `--attempt <id>` remains the next serial PR.

## What changed

- add a closed `repair-attempt-<24 hex>` operational identity;
- retain the concrete repository root, exact 40-character HEAD, producer version, selected seam, and creation telemetry;
- copy the existing workflow manifest, commands, brief, before snapshot, and packet into an attempt-specific directory;
- bind each copy by role, repo-relative path, byte count, and SHA-256 digest;
- publish artifact copies and `attempt.json` through temporary-file + sync + rename operations;
- reject source artifacts outside the selected root, duplicate roles, duplicate destination names, malformed IDs, malformed manifests, and attempt-directory reuse;
- hook persistence only after a successful `agent repair --phase before` execution;
- prove the after phase and unrelated commands cannot create a new attempt;
- add schema `0.1` and the current contract documentation.

## Current user journey

```text
ripr agent repair --phase before
-> existing before-phase artifacts complete
-> target/ripr/repair-attempts/<id>/attempt.json
-> state = awaiting_edit
-> human/external agent edits one focused test
-> existing --seam-id ... --phase after compatibility route
```

The CLI prints the attempt ID, manifest path, and the exact currently supported next command after persistence succeeds.

## Why this boundary

The current driver writes repository-global artifacts under `target/ripr/workflow`; a later before invocation can replace them. This PR preserves those compatibility paths but also takes attempt-specific copies before returning success, so one prepared attempt has durable bytes and identity for the later finish/resume slices.

## Proof included

Focused tests cover:

- closed attempt-ID syntax and rejection;
- atomic manifest round-trip with no temporary residue;
- attempt-specific artifact copying and digest binding;
- CLI selection of only the successful before phase for persistence;
- no new attempt from `--phase after` or unrelated commands.

Hosted exact-head checks remain the execution authority for formatting, Clippy, unit/integration tests, schema/output contracts, packaging, Windows behavior, and repository policy.

## Release disposition

Post-`0.11.0`. This PR must not enter or delay the active immutable-candidate train unless #2766 explicitly changes scope.

## Deliberate residuals

- `--phase after` does not yet accept `--attempt`;
- after/verify/receipt are not yet rebound to the manifest;
- `agent status` does not yet enumerate multiple attempts;
- edit-cage and exact fix-site fields remain authoritative in the retained packet rather than duplicated into manifest schema `0.1`;
- stale, incomparable, terminal movement, replay, and tamper transitions remain PR B/C work.

## Non-goals

- no automatic test or source edit;
- no shell hook or edit command;
- no verification-command or mutation execution authority;
- no support-tier promotion;
- no current release candidate, source promotion, version, tag, publication, signing, marketplace, or credential operation.

## Relationships

- #2927 — parent durable-attempt issue;
- #2443 — delivered compatibility two-phase driver;
- #1617 — typed command authority; no route is reconstructed from display prose;
- #2928 — future VS Code shell over this shared attempt;
- #2929 — future packaged CLI/VSIX parity proof;
- #1702 / #1560 / #1579 — future real-attempt consumers.

Refs #2927.
